course: dictionary
lesson: 12827
title: Strong and clear words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Emphatic means said or done strongly and clearly, so that everyone knows you really mean it. «No!» said loudly and firmly is an emphatic no.
explain: You can hear emphasis in speech and see it in writing. Writers show it with exclamation marks, bold letters or words like «really». Body language can be emphatic too, like a hand banging on the table.
explain: In sport news, an emphatic victory is a very clear win by a lot. «The team won emphatically, 6 goals to 1.» The big score shows the win was not close. Check it: swap in strong and clear, and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Emphatic is an adjective, and emphatically is the adverb: «She emphatically shook her head.»
fact: «Emphatic» means said or done strongly and clearly, to show you really mean it.
fact: An emphatic victory is a very clear win by a lot.
fact: Writers can make writing emphatic with exclamation marks, bold letters or italics.
fact: Phrases to know: emphatic no, emphatic victory, emphatically deny.
exercise: «She gave an emphatic no when asked to share.» What does emphatic mean here? | *Strong and clear, showing she really meant it | Happy and excited | Quiet and unsure | Sleepy and slow
exercise: The team won the match emphatically, 6 goals to 1. What does this show? | *A very clear win by a lot | A draw with no goals | A close game that went to the last minute | A game that was cancelled
exercise: «The boy shook his head emphatically.» Find and copy the word that tells how he shook his head. | *emphatically | boy | head | shook
test: Which of these makes writing more emphatic? | Hiding the important words | *Using bold letters and exclamation marks | Using small, faint writing | Writing in a whisper
test: «The judge emphatically denied the rumour.» What does emphatically mean here? | Sadly, with tears | Late, after everyone else | Quietly, in a whisper | *Strongly and clearly, showing it was definitely not true
exam: Which is an emphatic victory? | A win by one point after extra time | *A very clear win by a lot | A game with no winner | A match that was postponed
